preConditionHook:
// Each of the last barCount bars has a STRICTLY SMALLER high-low range than
// the bar before it -- trading interest drying up, the bar-scale companion to
// Grimes's reluctant-highs reading, which the book states beside it ("the bars
// are becoming smaller ... no more large trend bars"), so the two are meant to
// be placed together.
//
// DIRECTION-AGNOSTIC: a range carries no direction, so both sides are the same
// text. That is the difference from ShrinkingBodies, which compares BODIES and
// ALSO requires every bar of the run to close with the trend -- a run of
// shrinking ranges with mixed closes fires here and not there, and no
// parameter of that rule drops its body-colour term. Not
// ShrinkingConsecutiveRanges, which compares the SPANS of two adjacent
// FIXED-LENGTH WINDOWS rather than bar-to-bar ranges; not NarrowRangeBar, one
// bar against a window mean.
//
// All-of-N written the way ShrinkingBodies writes it -- start true and let any
// failing pair clear the flag -- so the loop runs unconditionally and
// EasyLanguage's lack of short-circuiting cannot matter. The pair comparison
// is guarded by kk <= barCount - 2 INSIDE a loop that runs 0..barCount-1,
// rather than run as a loop to barCount - 2, so no loop is ever asked to run
// with a limit below its start (at barCount 1 the rule is vacuously true; the
// grid starts at 2).
//
// Ranges are compared as DIFFERENCES, never as a ratio: EL raises a runtime
// divide-by-zero and a zero-range bar is legal data. Differences only, so the
// rule passes the shift test.
//
// The flag is a class member and persists across bars; it is reset at the top
// of the hook rather than relied on to start true.
//
// Deepest bar touched: barCount - 1.
shrinking = true;
for (kk = 0; kk < (int)barCount; ++kk) {
    if (kk <= (int)barCount - 2) {
        if (el_ge(high[kk] - low[kk], high[kk + 1] - low[kk + 1])) shrinking = false;
    }
}
